ghpull:518 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "shacl: implement SHACL-SPARQL constraints (sh:sparql, §5-6)" ;
    bl:number 518 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:54 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr518 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:54 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-13T22:23:27+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-15T19:43:18Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr518Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #54 asked for SHACL-AF §5-6 (sh:sparql/sh:SPARQLConstraint and sh:target/sh:SPARQLTarget), the phase after SHACL Core's Datalog translation (Phases 1-3, already landed). These queries can express joins, negation and aggregates with no general Datalog equivalent, so they execute directly via sparql_parser against the un-materialised data graph, in a pass kept separate from the Datalog materialisation. A blocking discovery mid-implementation: sparql_parser only implements SPARQL's ?name variable form (VAR1), never the $name form (VAR2) that every SHACL-AF spec example uses for $this/$value/$path, even though a `predicate_is_var` check elsewhere tests for both sigils before delegating to a $-blind parser. Rather than extending the shared parser's grammar for this one caller, embedded query text is textually rewritten ($name -> ?name) before parsing. Malformed/failing sh:sparql constraint queries are a hard validate() Err (silently skipping one would make bad data look conforming); sh:target SPARQLTarget execution failures are warn-and-skip instead, since threading a Result through data_targets/eval_all/pre_compute_violations was out of scope for this PR (tracked as a follow-up, #522) — a pre-flight parse-check on every embedded query, run unconditionally regardless of focus-node count, at least catches the common typo/unsupported-syntax case up front." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:518 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr518Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr518 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr518Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "$this is pre-bound per focus node by re-executing each sh:sparql query once per node, injecting a single-row VALUES ($this) { (<node>) } at the front of the parsed query's where_clause (confirmed via sparql_parser::execute's join_solutions_with_values to be a real join, not a seed-and-ignore). focus_node on each resulting ValidationResult comes from the loop variable, not from the row's own $this binding, so a query that doesn't happen to project $this still attributes correctly. SPARQL target queries (sh:target SPARQLTarget) execute against the pristine data store, never the working/materialised store, in both places a target is resolved (translate::target_rules and lib.rs's data_targets) — by the time target computation runs in the full validate() pipeline, sh:closed and Phase-2 constraint evaluation have already written synthetic urn:dagalog:shacl:* triples into the working store, and an arbitrary SPARQL target pattern (unlike the fixed rdf:type/rdfs:subClassOf* pattern sh:targetClass uses) could easily match those and make the target set depend on evaluation order." ;
    agp:alternative "Batching every focus node into one query execution via a multi-row VALUES block was considered and rejected for sh:sparql SELECT/ASK constraints: it diverges from per-focus-node evaluation semantics whenever the query has its own LIMIT/OFFSET, or an aggregate not grouped by $this, since those would apply once to the whole batched result set instead of once per node. Kept as a possible follow-up optimisation (#521) if per-node re-execution proves to be a real bottleneck, since it is not equivalent in the general case." .
